    "doi": "https://doi.org/10.3389/fpls.2026.1811299",
    "abstract": "Elevated CO<sub>2</sub> partly mitigated the effects of warming; however, seasonal soil water availability remained the primary constraint on yield. The results demonstrated that the calibrated DSSAT-CROPGRO-Soybean model provides a reliable basis for predicting the adverse effects of future climatic conditions on soybean production, while multi-GCM climate projections indicated that the magnitude of these effects may vary substantially depending on emission scenario, projection period, and water availability.",
    "field": "Crop simulation modeling",
    "problem": "Predicting future soybean yields in the vulnerable Mediterranean region is challenging due to significant uncertainty arising from variability among global climate models and the complex interactions between climate change impacts, elevated CO\u2082, and different irrigation strategies.",
    "method": "This study uses a calibrated DSSAT-CROPGRO-Soybean model driven by a multi-model ensemble of bias-corrected climate projections to assess future soybean yield responses under different irrigation regimes and emission scenarios (RCP 4.5 and 8.5).",
    "task": "The task is to evaluate soybean yield responses in a Mediterranean region to future climate change and different irrigation regimes using the DSSAT-CROPGRO-Soybean model under multiple climate projections.",
